parsed_content.explanation:
The AddressSanitizer runtime error is a segmentation fault (SEGV) caused by a NULL pointer dereference in the unit test code. The root cause is the following lines in the unit test:

extern FILE * stdin = NULL;
extern FILE * stdout = NULL;
extern FILE * stderr = NULL;

These lines do not just declare the variables as extern, but also initialize them to NULL, which overrides the standard library's stdin, stdout, and stderr pointers. As a result, any call to printf (which writes to stdout) will attempt to use a NULL pointer, causing a segmentation fault. The fix is to remove the '= NULL' initializations, leaving only the extern declarations (or, more correctly, to remove these lines entirely, as stdin, stdout, and stderr are already defined by the C standard library and do not need to be redeclared). This is a minimal change that preserves the rest of the code structure.

